e award this year but how are they  going in terms of keeping gamers updated  with the development  of their games in this field my  immediate reaction is that they're not  doing too badly he opens up battlefield  hardline to gamers in advance via the  June public beta although hardline was  pushed back to 2015 this still backs up  EAS player first culture in a couple of  ways first up EA have said they don't  want to release again that isn't good  enough and the feedback from gamers who  play the shooter back in June suggested  that hard line could be improved it also  reinforces the idea that EA actually  give a crap what you thought of their  game which let's be honest they haven't  always seemed to in the past the final  product of battlefield hardline remains  to be seen as yet so let's talk he is  too big releases within the last three  months of EAS player first mentality the  sims 4 and NHL 15 now the sims 4 isn't a  bad game but it's certainly not the game  that sims fans were looking for my  initial thoughts upon hearing the EA had  dropped the game's open-world pulls  toddlers and customization options and  replaced these things with funny walks  and sim emotions suggested to me that EA  didn't understand what their fans wanted  and why they enjoyed the series The Sims  4 has some interesting new aspects like  the well integrated gallery filled with  custom content and the emotion focused  gameplay which can sometimes be a help  instead of a hindrance he did make a  good call by not pushing the sims 4  online like it did with SimCity an  unpopular decision the company  eventually altered to include an offline  mode but the nature of the game feels  like a betrayal of what myself and other  sims fans expected that is more not less  NHL is another story while we can  attribute this game and the sims force  issues with the fact that the  development existed long before the  player first mantra players still had a  huge problem with the hockey games  launched again NHL 15 for new gen  consoles isn't altogether a bad game  it's a game with some decent features  and some interesting new additions the  sure that gamers are having at the  moment is that the game is unfinished  much like the sims for NHL 15 is missing  many of the little features that fans  have come to enjoy and expect from the  series some fans were given early access  which is awesome but they were the first  to see all the missing features notable  absences on the xbox one and ps4  versions included the popular EA Sports  Hockey League and connected GM modes he  quickly conducted damage control with  NHL producer Sean Ramsey singh posting a  list of features the company intends to  patch back in in the coming months on  the other hand their latest installment  for that other popular sports franchise  which launched last week FIFA 15 seems  to be going down pretty damn well gamers  were able to get their hands on a demo  beforehand which also happens to be the  most played demo in the company's sports  divisions history EA are only three  months into their newly enforced player  first culture so it's fair to give them  some benefit of the doubt just at the  moment that's largely due to the fact  that the lion's share of the development  for this year's releases has taken place  long before the announcement of their  altered company attitude to players the  releases of Dragon Age Inquisition and  battlefield hardline are certainly going  to give us more of an idea of whether or  not the company is making a change on  top of that their recent interesting  keeping game is updated and making  dragon age and battlefield better don't  seem to be a positive adjustment for the  meantime either way will surely be  keeping an eye on where the company goes  from here but let me know what you think  what do you reckon EA can do to prove  themselves to gamers and you think they  can ultimately turn it around leave me  your thoughts and I will catch you next  week  you
 

